2. Mandatory Attribution

Any use, deployment, redistribution, publication, demonstration, integration,
derivative work, or service that incorporates or materially depends on the Software
must provide clear attribution to the original project and author.

The attribution must contain, at minimum:

  "ATAS — Added Time Assistance System. Created by José Francisco Bustamante
  Ocampo. Copyright 2026 José Francisco Bustamante Ocampo and ATAS contributors."

The attribution must not be removed, obscured, or presented in a manner that is
reasonably likely to make the authorship unclear.

3. User-Facing Deployments

If the Software or a derivative work has a graphical, web, mobile, command-line, or
other user-facing interface, the attribution required by Section 2 must be displayed
in a reasonably accessible location such as an About, Credits, Legal, System
Information, acknowledgements screen, startup banner, or equivalent location.

The attribution need not interfere with ordinary operation and need not appear on
every screen.

4. Headless, Embedded, API, and Internal Deployments

If no user-facing interface exists, the attribution required by Section 2 must be
included in the operator documentation and in at least one reasonably discoverable
machine or runtime surface, such as startup output, a legal/about endpoint, generated
documentation, or equivalent system information.

5. Source and Binary Redistribution

Redistributions of source code must retain this License, the copyright notice, the
NOTICE file, and the attribution requirements.

Redistributions in binary, container, appliance, hosted, or other non-source form
must reproduce this License or a link to the complete License and must preserve the
NOTICE and attribution in accompanying documentation or another reasonably
accessible legal/credits location.

6. Modified and Derivative Works

Modified files or derivative works must clearly state that they have been modified
from ATAS. Rebranding does not remove the attribution obligation. A derivative work
may identify its own authors in addition to, but not instead of, the attribution
required by Section 2.

